q('AQP4-IgG-positive NMOSD is primarily directed against which cellular target?', 'Aquaporin-4 on astrocytic endfeet',[
 ('Myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein on oligodendrocytes','This defines a different antibody-associated disorder.'),('Acetylcholine receptors at the motor endplate','These are relevant to myasthenia gravis.'),('Peripheral nodal neurofascin as its defining antigen','This is associated with selected peripheral autoimmune nodopathies.')],
 'NMOSD is an autoimmune astrocytopathy', '**AQP4 is highly expressed on astrocytic endfeet.**', ['Antibody-mediated complement injury can produce secondary demyelination and axonal damage.','This biological distinction helps explain why MS therapies are not interchangeable.'], '**Classify the antibody-defined disease accurately.**', ['MOGAD is distinct from AQP4-NMOSD.','Seronegative NMOSD requires a stricter clinicoradiological assessment.'], [['AQP4-NMOSD','Astrocytic target'],['MOGAD','Myelin surface target'],['MS','Different disease framework']], [ND])
q('Severe transverse myelitis produces a central cord lesion spanning five vertebral segments. Which diagnostic consideration is especially important?', 'NMOSD, while also assessing MOGAD and other causes of longitudinally extensive myelitis',[
 ('Typical MS is proven by lesion length alone','Long central lesions are less typical of ordinary MS and are not disease-specific.'),('A peripheral neuropathy fully explains the intramedullary lesion','A cord lesion localizes to the CNS.'),('Long lesion length excludes inflammatory disease','NMOSD and other inflammatory disorders can cause this pattern.')],
 'LETM is a pattern rather than a final diagnosis', '**Longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis spans at least three contiguous vertebral segments.**', ['AQP4-NMOSD is a major cause.','MOGAD, sarcoidosis, infection, vascular and other disorders remain possible.'], '**Exclude compression and assess urgently.**', ['Use antibody testing and the overall brain, optic and systemic phenotype.','Imaging timing can alter apparent lesion length.'], [['LETM','At least three vertebral segments'],['Central severe lesion','Supports NMOSD consideration'],['Differential','Broader than one disease']], [ND], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Intractable hiccups and vomiting precede optic neuritis; MRI shows a dorsal medullary lesion. Which NMOSD core syndrome is suggested?', 'Area postrema syndrome',[
 ('A peripheral vestibular syndrome','This does not account for the dorsal medullary and optic inflammatory pattern.'),('Isolated gastroparesis as the unifying diagnosis','The neurological context supports a central emetic-region syndrome.'),('A pure cortical visual syndrome','The area postrema is in the dorsal medulla, not occipital cortex.')],
 'An apparently gastrointestinal symptom can localize centrally', '**The area postrema is an AQP4-rich circumventricular structure involved in emetic control.**', ['Persistent unexplained nausea, vomiting or hiccups can be an early NMOSD attack.','A supportive medullary lesion strengthens attribution.'], '**Do not dismiss symptoms solely as gastroenteritis.**', ['Evaluate alternative systemic causes and hydration.','Recognize associated optic, cord or brainstem attacks.'], [['Dorsal medulla','Area postrema'],['Symptoms','Hiccups/vomiting'],['Association','AQP4-NMOSD']], [ND], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which test is preferred when AQP4-NMOSD is suspected?', 'Serum AQP4-IgG using an appropriate cell-based assay',[
 ('Low-titer ELISA positivity as definitive proof regardless of phenotype','Assay specificity and the clinical context matter.'),('CSF total protein as a substitute for AQP4 testing','This is not antigen-specific.'),('Serum MOG-IgG as proof of AQP4 disease','MOG-IgG points toward a distinct disease when criteria are fulfilled.')],
 'Assay selection changes diagnostic accuracy', '**Serum cell-based assays are preferred for AQP4-IgG detection.**', ['Obtain samples before immunotherapy when feasible without delaying urgent treatment.','Unexpected low-positive results may need confirmation.'], '**Interpret a negative result in context.**', ['Treatment and assay sensitivity can affect detection.','Consider retesting with a suitable assay when suspicion remains high.'], [['Preferred specimen','Serum'],['Preferred method','Cell-based assay'],['Unexpected result','Clinical correlation and confirmation']], [ND])
q('AQP4-IgG is negative after one isolated optic neuritis attack. Is seronegative NMOSD established?', 'No; seronegative NMOSD requires additional stringent clinical and MRI criteria and exclusion of alternatives',[
 ('Yes, every AQP4-negative optic neuritis is NMOSD','This would misclassify many causes.'),('Yes, a single nonspecific white-matter focus is sufficient','Nonspecific imaging does not fulfill the required framework.'),('No, because seronegative NMOSD can never occur','The category exists but requires careful criteria-based assessment.')],
 'Antibody-negative disease has a higher diagnostic burden', '**Seronegative criteria require dissemination in space and specified core syndromes with MRI support.**', ['MOGAD should be evaluated in a compatible phenotype.','Infection, sarcoidosis and MS remain relevant alternatives.'], '**Avoid a lifelong immunotherapy label from one nonspecific event.**', ['Review testing method and treatment timing.','Seek expert reassessment when the pattern is uncertain.'], [['AQP4 positive','Different criteria pathway'],['AQP4 negative','Stricter clinicoradiological requirements'],['MOGAD','Separate diagnosis']], [ND])
q('An AQP4-positive NMOSD attack causes rapidly evolving paraplegia. What acute treatment principle is most appropriate?', 'Prompt high-dose corticosteroids with early plasma exchange for severe deficits or inadequate response',[
 ('Wait several weeks for spontaneous recovery before treatment','Attack-related injury can become permanent.'),('Use interferon beta as acute rescue','MS therapies are not interchangeable with NMOSD attack treatment.'),('Use oral maintenance azathioprine as the only immediate rescue','Its delayed preventive action does not replace acute treatment.')],
 'Attack timing influences disability', '**NMOSD disability is often attack-driven, so acute treatment should not be delayed.**', ['IV methylprednisolone is commonly used initially.','Early apheresis is particularly important for severe or poorly responding attacks.'], '**Plan relapse prevention after stabilization.**', ['Assess rehabilitation, bladder function and pressure-injury risks.','Do not use the good response of a prior mild attack to justify delay.'], [['Acute steroid','Rapid inflammatory suppression'],['Plasma exchange','Early escalation in severe attacks'],['Maintenance','Prevents future attacks']], [NT], 'clinical_scenario')
q('After a first confirmed AQP4-IgG-positive NMOSD attack, what is the usual long-term principle?', 'Offer relapse-preventive immunotherapy rather than waiting for a second disabling attack',[
 ('Withhold prevention because the first attack proves a monophasic course','AQP4-positive disease carries substantial relapse risk.'),('Use glatiramer as the universally preferred NMOSD prevention','It is not an established NMOSD preventive strategy.'),('Treat only MRI spots while ignoring clinical attacks','Clinical attack prevention is central.')],
 'Prevention begins before recurrent disability accumulates', '**NEMOS recommends long-term therapy after the first AQP4-positive attack.**', ['Options target complement, B cells or IL-6 pathways, with other immunosuppressive strategies in selected settings.','Choice depends on disease severity, comorbidity, access and safety.'], '**Differentiate prevention from acute rescue.**', ['Treatment onset can be delayed for some agents.','Bridging and monitoring should be individualized.'], [['First AQP4 attack','Consider long-term prevention'],['Drug choice','Individualized mechanism and safety'],['Outcome','Reduce future attacks']], [NT])
q('Which infection-prevention issue is particularly important before a terminal-complement inhibitor for NMOSD?', 'Meningococcal vaccination and any indicated antimicrobial prevention, with ongoing vigilance despite vaccination',[
 ('A tetanus vaccine alone fully addresses the risk','Complement blockade creates a particular meningococcal vulnerability.'),('Vaccination eliminates every meningococcal infection risk','Breakthrough infection can occur.'),('No infection counseling is needed because the target is not a lymphocyte','Terminal complement is important to antibacterial defense.')],
 'Mechanism predicts a specific infectious risk', '**C5 blockade impairs terminal-complement defense against meningococci.**', ['Follow the specific product and local vaccination/prophylaxis protocol.','Urgent treatment may require additional protective measures when vaccination timing is incomplete.'], '**Teach emergency symptom recognition.**', ['Fever, severe headache or systemic illness needs prompt assessment.','Continue vigilance during treatment rather than relying on a vaccination record alone.'], [['Mechanism','Terminal-complement inhibition'],['Risk','Meningococcal disease'],['Protection','Vaccination plus specified additional measures']], [NT])
q('Bilateral optic neuritis with prominent optic-disc swelling and perineural enhancement raises suspicion for which disorder?', 'MOG antibody-associated disease',[
 ('Typical MS solely because optic neuritis is present','The bilateral swollen-disc phenotype is a clue to an alternative.'),('Pure peripheral neuropathy','This does not explain optic nerve inflammation.'),('AQP4-NMOSD excluded solely by disc swelling','The phenotype favors MOGAD but does not absolutely exclude other causes.')],
 'Optic neuritis has antibody-associated phenotypes', '**MOGAD often involves the anterior optic nerve with disc edema and sometimes perineural enhancement.**', ['Bilateral attacks can occur.','AQP4 disease more often has severe posterior/chiasmal involvement, with overlap.'], '**Use phenotype-guided antibody testing.**', ['Confirm serum MOG-IgG with an appropriate cell-based assay.','Exclude alternative optic neuropathies.'], [['MOGAD clue','Disc edema/perineural pattern'],['AQP4 clue','Posterior/chiasmal involvement'],['Diagnosis','Antibody plus compatible syndrome']], [MOG,ND], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which approach best supports MOGAD diagnosis?', 'A compatible core demyelinating event, suitable serum MOG-IgG testing and exclusion of a better explanation',[
 ('Any low-positive result from indiscriminate screening','Low pretest probability increases false-positive attribution.'),('MOG-IgG positivity as a substitute for every clinical requirement','The clinical event and exclusion criteria remain necessary.'),('AQP4 positivity is required in every MOGAD case','The disorders have different defining antibodies.')],
 'Testing should follow the phenotype', '**The international criteria combine a core event with antigen-specific evidence.**', ['Use a validated cell-based assay.','The significance of low-positive or unspecified-titer results requires additional supportive features.'], '**Avoid broad-panel diagnosis by coincidence.**', ['Correlate optic, cord, brainstem, cerebral or ADEM features.','Consider retesting or confirmation when the result is discordant.'], [['Core event','Required clinical context'],['MOG-IgG','Appropriate assay'],['Low positive','Additional support needed']], [MOG])
q('A low-positive serum MOG-IgG result is found in an adult with a highly typical MS MRI pattern. What is the best next step?', 'Reassess pretest probability, assay validity and required supporting features rather than automatically relabeling the disease',[
 ('Treat the low-positive result as more definitive than all other evidence','False-positive attribution is a recognized concern.'),('Assume all MS patients also have MOGAD','The diseases are distinct and indiscriminate testing is misleading.'),('Ignore antibody testing in every optic neuritis case thereafter','Testing is valuable in compatible phenotypes.')],
 'A weak result needs stronger context', '**Positive predictive value falls when testing is used in an unlikely phenotype.**', ['International criteria specify extra support for low-positive results.','Review the exact assay and whether AQP4 testing is appropriate.'], '**Resolve discordance before changing long-term treatment.**', ['Seek expert radiological and laboratory review.','Do not equate analytical positivity with causal diagnosis.'], [['Low titer','Context-dependent'],['Typical MS pattern','Important competing evidence'],['Action','Confirm and apply criteria']], [MOG], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Does one MOGAD attack establish that lifelong relapse-preventive immunotherapy is necessary for every patient?', 'No; MOGAD can be monophasic or relapsing and long-term treatment is individualized',[
 ('Yes, the course is identical to AQP4-positive NMOSD in every patient','The natural histories and preventive evidence differ.'),('No, because MOGAD can never relapse','Relapsing optic neuritis and other recurrent attacks occur.'),('Only an asymptomatic MRI lesion proves a clinical relapse','Clinical relapse classification requires an attributable clinical event.')],
 'Separate diagnosis from future-course certainty', '**MOGAD has variable relapse behavior.**', ['Attack severity, recovery and subsequent course inform prevention decisions.','Persistent antibody positivity may inform risk but is not a deterministic forecast.'], '**Avoid a universal maintenance rule.**', ['Discuss uncertainty and arrange follow-up.','Recurrent or severe disease may justify preventive treatment through a specialist service.'], [['Course','Monophasic or relapsing'],['Maintenance','Individualized'],['Follow-up','Clinical recurrence and recovery']], [MOG])
q('After an infection a child develops encephalopathy with multifocal deficits and large poorly demarcated brain lesions. Which presentation is most consistent?', 'Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis, with MOGAD among the etiological considerations',[
 ('Typical isolated adult MS optic neuritis','The encephalopathic multifocal presentation differs.'),('Pure peripheral GBS','GBS does not explain the inflammatory brain lesions.'),('A diagnosis of MOGAD without antibody evidence','ADEM is a syndrome and does not alone establish MOGAD.')],
 'Encephalopathy helps define an ADEM phenotype', '**ADEM is a polyfocal inflammatory demyelinating syndrome with encephalopathy in the appropriate setting.**', ['Postinfectious timing is supportive but not mandatory.','MOG-IgG can identify an associated disease subgroup.'], '**Exclude infection and other mimics urgently.**', ['Treat significant inflammatory disease through specialist protocols.','Subsequent clinical events can change classification.'], [['ADEM','Encephalopathy plus multifocal disease'],['MOG-IgG','Possible etiological association'],['Course','Requires follow-up']], [MOG,AE], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which clinical tempo supports possible autoimmune encephalitis under the Graus framework?', 'Subacute progression under three months of memory, mental-status or psychiatric symptoms with supporting findings and exclusion of alternatives',[
 ('A lifelong unchanged personality trait alone','This lacks the required subacute disease pattern.'),('Any positive serum antibody despite no neurological syndrome','Antibody results need clinical interpretation.'),('A mandatory abnormal MRI in every case','MRI can be normal, especially early or in some subtypes.')],
 'Begin with the clinical syndrome', '**Possible AE requires a compatible subacute syndrome plus findings such as new seizures, focal signs, inflammatory CSF or suggestive MRI.**', ['Alternative causes must be reasonably excluded.','No single supporting finding is universal.'], '**Do not wait for every antibody result to assess the syndrome.**', ['Investigate infection and metabolic or toxic causes in parallel.','A criteria-based diagnosis guides urgency.'], [['Tempo','Less than three months'],['Support','Seizures, focal, CSF or MRI findings'],['Exclusions','Essential']], [AE])
q('Psychosis evolves into seizures, orofacial dyskinesia, reduced consciousness and autonomic instability in a young adult. Which antibody-associated syndrome is most characteristic?', 'Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis',[
 ('LGI1 encephalitis as the uniquely defining pattern','LGI1 more often features an older limbic/FBDS phenotype.'),('Pure primary psychiatric disease without further investigation','The seizures, dyskinesia and autonomic dysfunction require neurological assessment.'),('A peripheral autoimmune nodopathy','This does not explain the encephalitic syndrome.')],
 'Recognize a multisystem encephalitic progression', '**Psychiatric onset can precede the characteristic neurological syndrome.**', ['Movement abnormalities, speech dysfunction and dysautonomia are important clues.','CSF and EEG help assessment even when MRI is unremarkable.'], '**Address ICU risks and search for associated tumor.**', ['Screen for ovarian teratoma when appropriate.','Treatment includes immunotherapy and tumor treatment when present.'], [['Psychiatric onset','Can be early'],['Later features','Seizures, dyskinesia, dysautonomia'],['Association','Ovarian teratoma in a subset']], [AE,AT], 'clinical_scenario')
q('For suspected anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis, why should CSF antibody testing be included rather than relying only on serum?', 'CSF IgG antibodies to the GluN1 subunit have important diagnostic sensitivity and specificity in the appropriate syndrome',[
 ('Serum positivity always establishes encephalitis without a phenotype','Isolated serum results can be misleading.'),('CSF is never positive when serum is negative','Discordance can occur.'),('All antibody isotypes and targets are diagnostically interchangeable','The specified IgG and antigen matter.')],
 'Specimen and antibody specificity affect interpretation', '**Paired serum and CSF testing is often appropriate.**', ['Some patients have relevant CSF antibodies despite negative serum.','Low-confidence serum results should not override an incompatible clinical picture.'], '**Use validated antigen-specific testing.**', ['Interpret alongside CSF, EEG, MRI and clinical evolution.','A positive result does not eliminate investigation of concurrent infection.'], [['CSF GluN1 IgG','Key NMDA-receptor evidence'],['Serum alone','Can mislead'],['Diagnosis','Syndrome plus valid testing']], [AE])
q('An older adult has dozens of brief unilateral face-arm dystonic seizures, followed by memory decline and hyponatremia. Which antibody is most likely?', 'LGI1-IgG',[
 ('AQP4-IgG','This usually accompanies NMOSD core syndromes.'),('Acetylcholine-receptor IgG','This is associated with neuromuscular transmission disease.'),('MOG-IgG','This defines a demyelinating disease rather than the classic FBDS-limbic pattern.')],
 'FBDS is an important early immune-treatment clue', '**Faciobrachial dystonic seizures strongly suggest LGI1 encephalitis in the appropriate context.**', ['Hyponatremia and limbic memory dysfunction add support.','Routine EEG or CSF may be nondiagnostic.'], '**Do not rely on antiseizure medication alone.**', ['Prompt immunotherapy can control seizures and may limit cognitive injury.','Assess sodium and seizure-related safety.'], [['FBDS','LGI1 clue'],['Hyponatremia','Frequent association'],['Treatment','Immune and seizure management']], [AE,AT], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Probable autoimmune encephalitis is strongly suspected after appropriate samples and initial infection assessment. What is the best treatment principle?', 'Begin suitable first-line immunotherapy when justified rather than waiting weeks for antibody results, while addressing infectious alternatives',[
 ('Wait for every antibody panel regardless of deterioration','This can delay treatment of a serious immune-mediated disorder.'),('Give immunosuppression without assessing infection','Infections can mimic AE and be worsened by treatment.'),('Use antiseizure drugs as the only disease-directed therapy','They treat seizures rather than the underlying immune process.')],
 'Urgency and diagnostic caution must coexist', '**First-line approaches include corticosteroids, IVIG or plasma exchange according to context.**', ['Maintain empiric antimicrobial treatment when indicated pending infectious exclusion.','Select therapy with the patient’s severity and contraindications in mind.'], '**Escalate thoughtfully if response is inadequate.**', ['Rituximab or other second-line treatment may be appropriate.','Provide seizure, autonomic and rehabilitation care.'], [['First-line immune therapy','Steroid, IVIG or exchange'],['Infection','Parallel evaluation/treatment'],['Poor response','Specialist escalation']], [AT], 'clinical_scenario')
q('How should malignancy screening be approached in suspected autoimmune encephalitis?', 'Use an age-, phenotype- and antibody-informed tumor search, with follow-up when indicated',[
 ('A normal first scan permanently excludes an associated tumor','Occult tumors can require further or repeated evaluation.'),('Every antibody has the same tumor association','Associations vary substantially.'),('Tumor treatment is irrelevant once immunotherapy begins','Removing an associated tumor can be essential to recovery.')],
 'A paraneoplastic association changes treatment', '**The tumor search should fit the syndrome and the expected association.**', ['Ovarian teratoma is relevant in a subset of NMDA-receptor cases.','Other antibodies can suggest different malignancies and mechanisms.'], '**Coordinate neurology and oncology.**', ['Treat the tumor when found.','The screening interval depends on risk and initial findings.'], [['Phenotype/antibody','Guides search'],['Negative first search','Not always final'],['Tumor present','Treat alongside immune disease']], [AE,AT])
q('Does a normal initial brain MRI exclude autoimmune encephalitis?', 'No; some antibody-associated encephalitides have normal or nonspecific MRI findings',[
 ('Yes, an enhancing temporal lesion is mandatory in every subtype','The imaging phenotype is variable.'),('Yes, provided serum sodium is normal','Sodium is not an exclusion test.'),('No, therefore MRI has no role in evaluation','MRI remains important for support and alternative diagnoses.')],
 'Normal imaging is not equivalent to normal brain function', '**The clinical syndrome, EEG and CSF can provide evidence despite normal MRI.**', ['Repeat imaging may be useful as the illness evolves.','MRI helps identify vascular, infectious, neoplastic and other mimics.'], '**Require positive clinical reasoning rather than diagnosis by exclusion alone.**', ['Apply the criteria and use validated antibody tests.','Reassess when the course is discordant.'], [['Normal MRI','Does not exclude AE'],['EEG/CSF','Complementary evidence'],['Alternative causes','Still investigate']], [AE])
q('A broad serum panel returns an unexpected low-confidence neural antibody in a patient with a longstanding nonprogressive complaint. What is the best interpretation?', 'Assess the assay, specimen and phenotype before attributing the complaint to autoimmune encephalitis',[
 ('Any panel positivity mandates immunosuppression','False-positive attribution can cause serious harm.'),('All neural antibodies have equal disease specificity','Targets, titers, isotypes and assay methods differ.'),('Clinical course is irrelevant after testing','The syndrome and tempo remain central.')],
 'Avoid antibody-driven misdiagnosis', '**A result has meaning within a compatible neurological syndrome.**', ['Confirm unexpected results with an appropriate laboratory strategy.','Some serum-only findings have limited specificity.'], '**Revisit the original clinical question.**', ['Look for objective evidence and plausible alternatives.','Do not expose patients to prolonged immunotherapy solely from an incidental panel result.'], [['Pretest probability','Changes predictive value'],['Assay/specimen','Affects validity'],['Treatment','Requires integrated evidence']], [AE,AT])
q('Leptomeningeal enhancement and cranial neuropathies occur with biopsy-proven systemic sarcoidosis. After mimics are excluded, what consensus category is supported without neural biopsy?', 'Probable neurosarcoidosis',[
 ('Definite neurosarcoidosis solely from serum ACE','Definite classification requires appropriate nervous-system pathological confirmation.'),('Possible disease only despite compatible systemic histology','Systemic histology supports the probable category in a compatible neurological syndrome.'),('MS proven by enhancement alone','Leptomeningeal and cranial-nerve disease require a broader differential.')],
 'Diagnostic confidence depends partly on biopsy location', '**Nonneural histology can support probable neurosarcoidosis when neurological findings fit.**', ['Neural histology supports definite disease.','Infection and malignancy must be actively considered.'], '**Seek a safe tissue target.**', ['Systemic imaging can reveal accessible nodes or other sites.','Neither serum nor CSF ACE alone is sufficient proof.'], [['Possible','Compatible, no pathological confirmation'],['Probable','Systemic pathology plus compatible neuro disease'],['Definite','Neural pathological confirmation']], [NS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has disabling inflammatory neurosarcoidosis with relapse during steroid taper. What long-term principle is most appropriate?', 'Consider steroid-sparing immunotherapy, with selected refractory cases receiving agents such as infliximab after safety assessment',[
 ('Repeated indefinite high-dose steroids without toxicity review','Cumulative toxicity and disease control require a longer-term plan.'),('Interferon beta as a universal sarcoid treatment','This is not a standard neurosarcoidosis strategy.'),('No treatment unless serum ACE becomes elevated','ACE is neither a mandatory activity marker nor a reliable treatment threshold.')],
 'Protect neurological function while limiting steroid harm', '**Glucocorticoids are commonly initial therapy, but recurrent disease may need a steroid-sparing agent.**', ['Methotrexate and other agents are used according to phenotype and severity.','TNF inhibition can be useful in selected severe or refractory disease.'], '**Screen before substantial immunosuppression.**', ['Assess infection risks including TB and hepatitis as appropriate.','Reconfirm diagnosis if the response or trajectory is atypical.'], [['Steroid','Initial inflammatory control'],['Steroid-sparing drug','Reduce relapse/toxicity burden'],['Refractory disease','Selected biologic therapy']], [NST], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Meningoencephalomyelitis with radial perivascular enhancement extending from the ventricles raises suspicion for GFAP astrocytopathy. Which test most supports the diagnosis?', 'CSF GFAP-IgG with appropriate confirmatory testing and clinical correlation',[
 ('An isolated nonspecific serum ANA','This does not establish the antigen-specific neurological disorder.'),('Serum AQP4-IgG as proof of GFAP disease','AQP4 and GFAP define different antibody associations.'),('Radial enhancement alone as pathognomonic proof','The pattern is characteristic but not sufficient without excluding mimics.')],
 'A characteristic enhancement pattern guides testing', '**GFAP astrocytopathy can present with meningitis, encephalitis and myelitis.**', ['CSF antibody testing is more specific in the appropriate inflammatory phenotype than isolated serum positivity.','The radial enhancement pattern is not universal.'], '**Investigate alternative and associated disorders.**', ['Infection and neoplasia can mimic inflammatory disease.','Treatment often involves corticosteroids with further therapy individualized to course.'], [['MRI clue','Radial perivascular enhancement'],['Test','CSF GFAP-IgG'],['Interpretation','Phenotype and exclusion of mimics']], [GF], 'clinical_scenario')

